From: Sweet port <sweet@localhost>
Subject: [PATCH] ufs: look up "sweet-ufs" without a leading slash

SecureDTLookupEntry resolves a path from the root and rejects a leading
slash: upstream calls it as "chosen/memory-map", never "/chosen/memory-map".
sweet_ufs_init asked for "/sweet-ufs", so the lookup failed with
kErrorNotFound even though the node was present in the ADT, and every boot
died in

    panic: IOFindBSDRoot: SWEET UFS root ufsroot0 unavailable: 6

6 is ENXIO, from the "no node in the device tree" branch. QEMU instruction
tracing confirmed the driver body never executed: the only PCs inside its
address range were memcpy, and _sweet_ufs_rootdev itself was never entered,
which is what a clean early return looks like.

Found by disassembling _sweet_ufs_rootdev, which at +0x80 calls
SecureDTLookupEntry and branches to its ENXIO return at +0x198 when the
result is not kSuccess. The node was independently verified present in the
payload's ADT, so the tree was fine and only the path string was wrong.

This is deliberately a separate patch applied after the ufs series rather
than an edit to 0028: 0030, 0034, 0047, 0054, 0060, 0069, 0072, 0077 and
others carry line-numbered hunks into bsd/dev/sweet_ufs.c, and shifting the
file inside 0028 made 18 later patches fail to apply.

---
 bsd/dev/sweet_ufs.c | 4 ++--
 1 file changed, 2 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/bsd/dev/sweet_ufs.c b/bsd/dev/sweet_ufs.c
--- a/bsd/dev/sweet_ufs.c
+++ b/bsd/dev/sweet_ufs.c
@@ -480,8 +480,8 @@ sweet_ufs_init(void)
 	int error;
 
-	if (SecureDTLookupEntry(NULL, "/sweet-ufs", &node) != kSuccess ||
+	if (SecureDTLookupEntry(NULL, "sweet-ufs", &node) != kSuccess ||
 	    SecureDTGetProperty(node, "reg", (void const **)&reg, &size) != kSuccess ||
 	    size < 2 * sizeof(uintptr_t)) {
-		printf("SWEET UFS: no /sweet-ufs node in the device tree\n");
+		printf("SWEET UFS: no sweet-ufs node in the device tree\n");
 		return ENXIO;
 	}
